license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/erenup/tridiagonal-spectral-separation"
abstract: >-
  For every n >= 2, every 0 < delta < 1/2 and every Hermitian tridiagonal
  n x n matrix T with ||T||_2 <= 1, a deterministic algorithm selects t in
  [0, delta/n] such that D = tX, with X = diag(1, ..., n), satisfies
  ||D||_2 <= delta and gap(T + D) > 2^-34 delta^2 n^-12 >= 2^-34 (delta/n)^12.
  The construction uses a classical convex weighted eigenvalue sum and its
  finite-scale slope variation; a tridiagonal localization argument turns
  near-degenerate eigenvalues into separated responses to the fixed diagonal
  direction, yielding a quantitative gap certificate. An adaptive interval
  selector identifies a certifying window in at most ceil(log2(16 n^3))
  refinements. With an exact-arithmetic spectral-function oracle based on
  the determinant recurrence and Graeffe root-squaring, the algorithm uses
  O(n[1 + log(n/delta)]^3) arithmetic operations, comparisons and square
  roots, which answers the nearly linear deterministic Minami perturbation
  question KE-02 with a = 12, c_0 = 2^-34 and q = 3. Includes the paper, a
  Lean formalization of the finite and scalar core, exact supporting checks
  and the code and data of the numerical illustrations. Files outside paper/
  are licensed under MIT; files in paper/, including the manuscript source
  and PDF, are licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0.
